{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793691898,"nonce":"FzVdh0YUTUCbsTTGSyjAf2i0bZs=","title":"CI benchmark results do not record the runner's hardware, and the runners are not documented here","message":"GitHub documents the standard runners' vCPU count, memory and disk (ubuntu-latest: 4 vCPU, 16 GB, x64; macos-latest: 3 (M1), 7 GB, arm64; macos-15-intel: 4 vCPU, 14 GB) and says standard 2- and 4-core Linux runners may run on multi-cloud compute, AWS or Azure. It does not document the CPU model, hypervisor, whether a vCPU is a hardware thread, cache sizes or run-to-run consistency. Our result files recorded only the CPU model string, the logical CPU count and memory, which is not enough to test the SMT and cache hypotheses in c708f07 or to say which cloud a CPU came from.\n\nEach language result now also records hardware: DMI vendor and product (these name the cloud, and on some the instance type), hypervisor, threads per core, cores per socket, L2 and L3 (lscpu on Linux, sysctl on macOS), the pages show them next to the machine tab, and docs/ci-benchmark-machines.md states what GitHub documents, what it does not, and how to read the tabs.\n\nNot done: the ZynML kernel jobs record no hardware yet. Unverified in CI until a run after the push: check hardware.vendor, product and threads_per_core in the downloaded lang-zylua-ubuntu-latest artifact.","files":null}]}